From a9de4ff3962bf6165223426822e1339ac617d86c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 15:19:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] dvbridge: a screenshot or capture during native Dolby Vision
 output is of the picture

With native Dolby Vision output the window framebuffer holds the
transport: the picture packed for the HDMI link, 4:2:2 in an RGB
buffer with metadata in the low bits. A screenshot, the thumbnail of a
bookmark and every other capture read that framebuffer, and got the
transport, or what an earlier frame had left there.

The picture itself exists while a frame is composed: the full-precision
picture into which the video is reconstructed and the GUI blended,
before it is packed. It is now what a capture reads:

- CWinSystemBase says whether what is presented is in a framebuffer of
  its own (HasCaptureFramebuffer()), and gives it for the frame being
  drawn (GetCaptureFramebuffer()). The GBM window system answers with
  the picture of the Dolby Vision bridge while native output is on;
- a frame that a capture waits for is composed, like one with GUI
  elements: a frame drawn directly exists as the transport only. A
  request is taken up one frame at a time, so a screenshot costs the
  composing of one frame, and a consumer that captures every frame has
  every frame composed while it does (and, like frames with GUI
  elements, none of them is measured for the choice between the two
  ways of drawing);
- the composite tap (a screenshot) reads that framebuffer whole, at 16
  bits, and the video tap (a capture of the video only) takes its
  region from it, scaled by the GPU when another size is asked for. Its
  first row is the top one, where that of a window is the bottom one;
- such a capture is tagged BT.2020, PQ, full range: the picture as the
  stream gives it, before the display maps it;
- a frame that has no such picture (not composed, or not drawn) gives
  no capture, rather than the transport: the request fails as any
  capture that cannot be read does, with the line Kodi logs for it.

The picture is a floating-point texture. The read of a floating-point
framebuffer as GL_FLOAT, made for the half-float output of nvidia-drm,
is now also used where the picture is in one.

Taken from the idea of the Intel Dolby Vision LibreELEC fork (its
kodi-9999-native-screenshots.patch, which reads the same picture for
the composite tap); written anew for this tree, with the video tap.

Host test on a software OpenGL ES (Mesa 26.0.8, zink on lavapipe), with
Kodi's CaptureReadback.cpp, CaptureBlit.cpp and ScreenshotSurfaceGLES.cpp
compiled as they are (tests/capture_test.cpp): half-float and float
pictures are read at 16 and at 8 bits with the values they hold, those
outside [0, 1] clamped, their first row first; a screenshot is of the
whole picture; a capture of the video is of its region, at its size or
scaled, each quarter of a test picture where it belongs; a frame
without a picture gives nothing; and a window framebuffer is read as
before. That is the first run of the float read, and it is of a
framebuffer object: the branch for a floating-point window framebuffer
(the half-float output of nvidia-drm, read from GL_BACK) has still not
run anywhere. The stand-ins of the test give the picture a framebuffer
of their own making; the order in which Kodi draws, reads and presents
a frame is not run. Not run in Kodi nor on hardware: the read and its
conversion are done on the render thread, and how long they hold it
for a 3840x2160 float picture is not measured there.
